Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||12-01-2000||09:13 AM||||Homicidal,<P>Where are you located?<BR>All my cars do that below 40 degrees.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ThatRickGuy||12-01-2000||09:27 AM||||another thing it may be if you've just done some engine work is the lower intake. The aluminum intake on the iron block likes to loosen up a bit and may need to be retightened. My machinest/engine builder recommended that after a thousand miles or so I tear down the top of the intake and retorque all the bolts on the lower plenum to correct that.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Homicidal||12-01-2000||12:35 PM||||Valve seals......<P>I love the quick and accurate responses I get here, but keep in mind you are talking latin to me at this point.  I'm not a gear-head just yet, so I can't possibly fix this on my own.<P>I'm in San Diego, and when I think about it, I notice it's been doing it ever since it got colder down here.  Possibly?<P>As for fixing it, is it required?  Am I in danger if I let the problem persist (besides damaging the ozone  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)?  And is it a hard(expensive) fix?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||fldevel||12-01-2000||02:56 PM||||might take into consideration that the weather is cold now .. and it could be moisture building up in the exhaust <P>------------------<BR> "Dont spent too much time under your car or you will have lil fieros running around"||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||12-01-2000||07:58 PM||||When you said colder weather started it,it may be moisture.Is it smoke that disapates slowly,or is it steam that disappears very fast.take notice of exhaust vapors on other cars.If it's steam,you'll notice it immediately disappears.If that's your case,don't concern yourself.If you take a  lot of short trips You should run your engine long enough to remove all the moisture from your exhaust system to prevent premature corrosion problems. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ed||12-01-2000||08:12 PM||||White "smoke" is water-based.<P>Blue smoke is oil based.<P>Black smoke is gasoline based.  <P>If it is white and goes away, it is probably just condensation.  No problem.  If it always comes out it could be a cracked head or blown head gasket. But you said it goes away.  Good.   <P>Blue smoke on start-up is often due to worn valve seals.  I get a little of that, but I think it would have to be pretty bad to justify the expense of replacing them, at least by a mechanic.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Homicidal||12-01-2000||11:16 PM||||Yup, it's condensation and nothing more.  It's white, it dissapears almost immediately and only happens in the in colder, damp weather we've been having down here.<P>I know so because my car was sitting for a while today in the sun and since it was a bit warmer there was no smoke at all.<P>Thanks guys, for putting my mind to rest!||||reg||1
